00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.189 align:start position:0%
 
this is timor saying reading for  reviewer stephen petit metroid dread is  a triumphant return for the iconic  bounty hunter samasaran in large part  because it remains true to its lineage  like seeing an old friend for the first  time in many years metroid dread is  charmingly familiar an old-school side  scroller with a modern look and feel  dread played similarly to samus returns  for the nintendo 3ds developer mercury  steam brought over the excellent counter  attack mechanic to dread as well as the  manual sighted aiming system that gives  you pinpoint control over beam shots and  missiles while dread is pleasantly  familiar mechanically samus has never  felt this good to control with a pep in  her step and tight controls metroid  dread is silky smooth in motion an  absolute joy to play  utilizing the same 2.5 d art style from  samus returns metroid dread offers  crisper more detailed visuals that help  make each of the locales and their  enemies stand out rocky tunnels molten  caverns industrial compounds underwater  depths metroid red is filled with the  distinct level designs that have marked  a series series's storied history  improved animations especially from  samus herself and bosses enhance the  overall presentation while adhering to  the signature art direction and style  the series is known for  [Music]  metroid red has been billed as the swan  song of the current samus saga bringing  a story arc that began 35 years ago to a  satisfying close was always gonna be a  tall task but dread definitely untangles  the series's web of narrative mysteries  to deliver an affecting tale that  features some of the best writing and  atmospheric storytelling in the series's  history to get there dread kicks off  with samus arriving at a destination to  investigate a mission gone awry only to  soon find herself devoid of her decked  out suite of abilities this time it's  planet zdr where the federation has sent  a robotic task force called emmy to  search for evidence that the ex-parasite  lived on after the events of metroid  fusion samus quickly encounters a  powerful chozo warrior who knocks her  unconscious she awakens deep beneath the  planet's surface far away from her ship  with only her power beam and a batch of  missiles dred flips the script by  forcing samus to travel up and back to  her ship but in practice this change  isn't a meaningful shake-up as you'd  come to expect dread requires frequent  backtracking so you're moving in all  different directions throughout the  adventure one notable new feature that  makes both exploration and combat  different than usual from the start is  samus's ability to slide this allows her  to access areas previously only  accessible via morph ball as well as  provide a nimble way to dodge attacks  the morph ball is still needed in many  instances though the winding tunnel  system in each of the themed locales  inevitably leads samus into trippy  portals signifying the start of an emmy  zone surprising no one the robots sent  to the planet to investigate have been  reprogrammed to do what all  well-intentioned robots secretly want to  do kill emmy zones introduce stealth to  the metroid formula and it's a mostly  positive addition the emmy which crawl  on all fours along walls reconfiguring  physically to chase you through spaces  you'd think they can't navigate are  positively creepy inspiring the dread  the title alludes to if an enemy spots  you a chase sequence commences and if  you aren't able to outrun it it can be  instant game over you have a very brief  window to execute a perfect counter  which stuns the emmy and gives you an  opportunity to slide under it and escape  but the timing is extremely tight and  the window seems to vary to ensure  escape after capture feels like a stroke  of fortune pretty early on you unlock a  useful clocking ability that makes you  invisible which can be used to evade  emmy and pass through motion locked  doors but like the counter it isn't  enough to trivialize the emmy  nevertheless exploring an enemy  patrolled zone can be nerve-wracking but  it's never nearly as terrifying as it  could have been mostly because the  stakes are low if you get caught you  simply get sent back to the entrance  emmys wind up as more of a cumbersome  obstacle at times mainly because the map  layer often forces you to zigzag through  emmy zones to make progress and  backtrack coming across an emmy guarding  the exact exit you're searching for can  be tiresome especially since the  encounters are mostly the same  throughout in isolation emmys bring a  well-designed new element of gameplay  into the metroid fold but in the grand  scheme of things they can get in the way  of what metroid is all about careful and  thorough exploration  inside each zone is a floating orwellian  eyeball mini boss that once you're able  to find can be destroyed to receive a  one use omega cannon the process for  killing an enemy has two steps exposing  its head by shooting away the shield  with a machine gun and then charging up  the cannon to deliver the kill shot the  kill sequence is a clever puzzle of  sorts which is presented from an over  the shoulder camera perspective you have  to find a clear enough space in the zone  to shoot the enemy at a distance and  make it vulnerable otherwise you have to  start the process over from a different  spot with each successive emmy encounter  the process for killing then becomes  slightly trickier thanks to  environmental obstacles that force you  to think and act quickly despite the  intermittent annoyance the  moment-to-moment gameplay evolving emmys  is still enjoyable even if it feels  somewhat at odds with metroid's core dna  thankfully emmys aren't an overbearing  presence they are more of an aside the  vital framework of metroid is still  intact and dread handles exploration and  backtracking better than any other game  in the series thus far dread feels like  a more adventurous take on fusion's  cadence you still get mission updates  from the ai adam in network centers but  very rarely does it tell you exactly  where to go instead there's a clever  nudge in the right direction while still  giving you the freedom to explore though  i did get lost for a brief bit twice  throughout the adventure once i figured  out where to go i started to recognize  the subtle suggestions in both writing  and environment that point you in the  right direction dread doesn't hold your  hand it feeds you drips of information  and new abilities to allow you to  gradually explore previously unreachable  areas and exploring is the name of the  game here dread has more secrets to  uncover than its predecessors there are  missile packs all over the place  including upgrades that add a whopping  10 missiles to your ammunition capacity  dread might actually have too many  missile packs as i eventually had so  many that i didn't even need to be  concerned with being accurate with my  shots during late-game boss battles  energy tanks on the other hand remain  critical finds in addition to full  energy tanks mercury steam has added  energy tank pieces  four make a full energy tank quadrants  of the map with hidden collectibles  flash after you've visited them to tell  you there's something to be found but  not in a way that makes finding upgrades  any less rewarding speaking of the  in-game map it's deliciously detailed  marking every single location where you  picked up or saw an upgrade as well as  points of interest and doors that  require specific weapons to open you can  highlight specific icons as well as drop  pins at spots you want to circle back to  while the map can get a tad busy as you  make significant progress it does help  make the backtracking even after you  finish the story a worthwhile and  engaging endeavor of course exploration  is tied to samus's abilities and dread  features a plethora of upgrades old and  new there are more than 20 suit weapon  and ability upgrades in dread and almost  all of them are required to fully  explore the map for instance the various  suit allows samus to withstand heat in  fiery areas while the wide beam opens  certain hatches and moves large boxes  it's not uncommon to find multiple  upgrades in an hour your means of  exploring evolves quickly consistently  giving you more reasons to retrace your  steps and voyage to new sections that  said because of this constant barrage of  new upgrades it sometimes feels like you  barely make use of your newly acquired  gadgets this is especially the case for  beam upgrades which replace your  existing beam one of the new morph ball  gizmos is only really required once  immediately after finding it so  sometimes it can feel like dread really  suffers from a gluttony due to uneven  pacing of unlocks that clutters late  game sections dreads handful or so of  locales are wonderfully designed to test  both your platform and combat skills and  each has its own stable of enemies that  pack a hearty punch samus returns really  elevated metroid's combat with counters  and dread continues that many enemies  from flying monsters to hulking land  beasts have an attack that can be  counted and successfully slaying a  monster following a counted attack drops  more health or missile resources  rewarding you for making use of this  vital mechanic dread adds to the series  history of great boss fights with some  of the most daunting duels of samus's  bounty hunting career the best boss  battles like the showdown with a chained  up larger-than-life crate make you feel  small and powerless at first tasking you  with figuring out how to evade an  onslaught of attacks timing counters and  laying into the beasts with a flurry of  missiles  dread does sometimes fall into the same  rut as samus returns with repetition of  many bosses albeit not such to an  extreme degree more than anything else  metroid dread feels like going back to a  place of comfort after a long time away  though the gameplay is refined and new  features have been added to the mix  dread sticks closely to the formula of  his predecessors in the end for long  time fans like myself that's probably  for the best there's nothing to dread  here we're home again  [Music]  you

